; EXERCISE 1: TRUTH AND LIES
;
; The island of knights and knaves: theory/island.kurt describes it. Read it first.
;
; Prove each statement: replace the `todo` of its proof by your proof. Don't change the `show`
; lines. Check the file with
;
;     kurt 1-truth-and-lies.kurt
;
; until it says `Proof checked` (with `todo`s left it says `Proof almost checked`).

load theory/island
const A
prop S

; (a) What a knight says is true.
;     Hint: `assume` the left side; its parts `A says S` and `knight(A)` are facts of their own
;     when you write them as lines. What does the rule "says" give?
show (A says S) ∧ knight(A) ⇒ S
proof
    todo
qed

; (b) What a knave says is false.
show (A says S) ∧ knave(A) ⇒ ¬S
proof
    todo
qed

; (c) Whoever says something true is a knight.
show (A says S) ∧ S ⇒ knight(A)
proof
    todo
qed

; (d) No islander can say "I am a knave".
;     Hint: assume that A says it. Then knight(A) ⇔ knave(A): split into the cases knight(A) and
;     ¬knight(A) (`case`), and reach `false` in both.
show ¬(A says knave(A))
proof
    todo
qed

; (e) Think, without Kurt: if A says "I am a knight", what do we learn about A? Why can't you
;     prove `knight(A)`, or `knave(A)`, from `A says knight(A)`?
